# is no default: they are the settings the Scala entry fixed (fallback
# dispatch, the closure backend, the instance table with trial 1024, the
# JIT's compilation budget of 10000, the profitable checkpoints, no native
# blocks, applications taken apart one layer at a time) and the reading
# options of round 28 (the instance table's complete tables never dropped,
# https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1790; a function applied to
# its continuation by the β rule as it is, not through the closed pack of
# https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1794; every weak-head
# table a reading leaves kept for the next reading, never dropped,
# https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1781; the complete tables
# in the reading's state alone, not in the cache service); the standard
# indexer, the higher-order
# saturator, the narrow block extractor (enumeration bound 256, whole fold
# bound 1, as measured: com.yang_bo.gezi.strategies.JointReading.measured), the baseline
# lemma encoding and the
# closures' e-matching).
# The executor that reads the programs is solo's tiered executor (the module
# of corpora/modules/solo_tiering, com.yang_bo.gezi.contest.modules.solo.TieringModule,
# https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1827), with the compilation
# by the executor (compilation_place by_executor): each program is read by
# the framework's JIT within 20000 evaluator steps, and compiled by the
# rounds below and read again only when that reading does not finish in them.
# Measured 2026-11-21 (the judge's supervisor on round 27's families
# regenerated from main's generators and its public seed, 122 evaluation
# points; CPUs 0 and 1; build 75a935c90; scored as round 28): 104.2 of 391
# against 99.3 for the rounds before every reading (99.2 and 99.6 in two
# other readings), and 99.1, 102.5 and 104.0 at 5000, 10000 and 40000 steps.
# Measured 2026-11-21 (the judge's supervisor,
# judgeSupervisor, on round 27's 238 public points as
# main 3b1dff6ef's judge rewrote them, 777f16225; CPUs 8 and 9, build
# 8ebc96e4e, 1-minute load 10.1 to 13.5 at the two readings' starts and
# ends; scored as round 28, hot rungs at 10): these reading options 113.4
# of 431 against 109.7 for the options before them (instance generations
# of 2^19, the closed pack, no table kept). Every family answered the same
# points but repeated_library_edit_distances_small,
# repeated_library_longest_common_subsequences_small and
# repeated_library_smallest_teams_small, which end at their budget either
# way: 2 of 6 against 3, 2 against 1, 5 against 6. Of the 48 families both
# finished, 39 faster by more than 5% and 9 slower, the sum of their wall
# times 129.5 s against 157.9 s.
# Measured 2026-11-20, 23:45 to 23:50 UTC (the same supervisor, CPUs 8 and
# 9, build 8ebc96e4e, on 36 points of eight of round 27's families
# regenerated from its public seed by the judge's judgePointsFile):
# with tables kept and continuations substituted, keeping the complete
# tables in the cache service (table_sharing strong) answered 2 of the six
# repeated_library_smallest_teams_small points where the reading state
# alone answered all six, so they stay local.
#
# The library is the base's for the lemma-compiled route (the setter's survey
# 1 ruling (1): the base is open to both entries, its lineage wren's). Its
# native typing comes through presets, never by importing a typing module
# alone (rule 43 of docs/design/gezi-contest-hard.md): the Prolog
# backend's types through corpora/compiler/prolog_backend_preset, the binary
# naturals through wren's corpora/compiler/wren_binary_preset (its library
# member and three of its round members, wren's work) and solo's
# corpora/compiler/solo_binary_preset, whose typing runs on wren's backend.
# Two lemma rounds on one e-graph, each run to its fixed point. The first
# saturates the base's typed block, specialization and unfolded modules and
# solo's own module solo_own_loop_typing, which gives a first-order loop's
# binders the filters the narrow grammar reads (the loops of the inlined
# hot shape), without opening closures. The second saturates the
# specialization module again with closures opened, so that its static
# argument lemma reaches the nested shape's inner loop, written
# eta-expanded: the round structure of wren's entry (#1270, credited to
# wren). The last round's extraction's narrow blocks run as bytecode and
# the framework's JIT reads what is left. Measured 2026-11-13 (the 42-point
# round 25 subset, CPUs 4 and 5, against main b38134135 with one round):
# the six nested points compile and answer in 2.2 to 4.3 s, against 27.6
# and 31.8 s at 10k and stopped at 60 s above; no family answers fewer
# points. Opening closures in a single round of all four modules instead
# stopped the direct hot ladder and killed the nested points' JVM.
# Each round enables every built-in rule (`rules`), as every round did
# before the rules were opt-in. Its leaves are those of every knowledge
# file of the entry (a contribution to the slot leaves that reads every file's leaves), as they were when the leaves were
# the library's and not a round's; its lemmas are its own files'. The
# backend is the choice of the same files' knowledge, of which the
# backend's slots are read.

# The file is read by projection off the tree whose visible subtree is
# Gezi's directory (StandardLibrary.entryTerm), in the scope of
# gezi; it imports the knowledge files at its top, each file's
# tuple's fields bound by position
# (https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1509, section 10.2).
